1. Read Input from User

Use the read command.

a) Example

echo "Enter your name"

read NAME

echo "Welcome $NAME"

Output

Enter your name

Bilal

Welcome Bilal

b) Read Multiple Inputs
echo "Enter first name and last name"

read FIRST LAST

echo $FIRST
echo $LAST

2. Read Password

Hide the typed password.

read -s PASSWORD

echo

echo "Password saved"

3. Prompt with read -p

Instead of using echo and read separately:

read -p "Enter your name: " NAME

echo $NAME

4. Read with Timeout

Wait only a few seconds.

read -t 5 -p "Enter name: " NAME

If the user does not enter anything within 5 seconds, read exits.

5. Default Value
NAME=${NAME:-Guest}

echo $NAME

If NAME is empty or unset, the output is:

Guest

6. Readonly Variable

Cannot be modified after declaration.

readonly COMPANY="DNEG"

echo $COMPANY

Trying to change it:

COMPANY="Google"

Output

bash: COMPANY: readonly variable

7. Unset Variable

Remove a variable.

CITY="Mumbai"

echo $CITY

unset CITY

echo $CITY

Output

Mumbai

The second echo prints nothing because the variable no longer exists.

8. Single Quotes vs Double Quotes
a) Double Quotes

Variables are expanded.

NAME="Bilal"

echo "Hello $NAME"

Output

Hello Bilal

b) Single Quotes

Variables are treated as plain text.

NAME="Bilal"

echo 'Hello $NAME'

Output

Hello $NAME

9. No Quotes
CITY=Mumbai

echo $CITY

Works fine for a single word.

If the value contains spaces:

CITY=New Delhi

This causes an error.

Correct:

CITY="New Delhi"

10. Concatenating Variables
FIRST="Bilal"

LAST="Ansari"

echo "$FIRST $LAST"

Output

Bilal Ansari
